WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:06.009
Here is a question people are often too polite to ask.

2
00:00:06.009 --> 00:00:09.931
If programmers write the code, what is all this other work about?

3
00:00:10.125 --> 00:00:13.550
Surely the job is done when the code is written.

4
00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:13.978
Right?

5
00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:16.347
Let's answer that one in a kitchen.

6
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.435
Picture yourself baking bread in your home kitchen.

7
00:00:19.435 --> 00:00:20.759
The neighbours love it.

8
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.169
Then the café down the street asks for forty loaves, every morning, starting Monday.

9
00:00:26.375 --> 00:00:29.253
Baking one good loaf was never the hard part.

10
00:00:29.253 --> 00:00:35.839
The hard part is forty good loaves, on time, every single morning, while you keep improving the recipe.

11
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.969
And nobody gets a burnt one.

12
00:00:38.625 --> 00:00:40.646
Software has the same problem.

13
00:00:40.646 --> 00:00:47.449
A program helps nobody until it runs for real people, every day, and keeps running after each change.

14
00:00:48.875 --> 00:00:50.780
Let's grow the bakery a little.

15
00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:54.222
You bake, and your cousin drives the loaves to the café.

16
00:00:54.375 --> 00:00:57.072
One Friday, the café calls at seven.

17
00:00:57.072 --> 00:00:58.420
The bread is flat.

18
00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:02.216
Your cousin can't fix it, because only you know the recipe.

19
00:01:02.216 --> 00:01:05.806
So you blame the hot van, and your cousin blames the dough.

20
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:06.917
And the café?

21
00:01:06.917 --> 00:01:07.975
Still no bread.

22
00:01:07.975 --> 00:01:11.503
The only thing that arrived on time was the blame.

23
00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:17.410
So the point is: with a wall in the middle, nobody owns the whole road from oven to shelf.

24
00:01:17.625 --> 00:01:20.812
Software companies were split the same way for years.

25
00:01:20.812 --> 00:01:25.382
Developers wrote the code, the instructions that tell a computer what to do.

26
00:01:25.625 --> 00:01:29.009
A second team, operations, ran that code on servers.

27
00:01:29.009 --> 00:01:34.214
A server is a computer that stays on day and night, and answers other computers.

28
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.258
When the app broke at two in the morning, operations got the call.

29
00:01:38.258 --> 00:01:40.764
But only the developers understood the code.

30
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.280
So both sides made releases rare.

31
00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:46.734
A release is one batch of changes handed to users.

32
00:01:46.734 --> 00:01:50.257
Rare releases grew huge, and everyone dreaded them.

33
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:55.456
Wouldn't it be nice if the person who knows the recipe also heard about the flat bread?

34
00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:57.066
That wish has a name.

35
00:01:57.066 --> 00:02:03.017
DevOps: a way of working where the people who build software also share the work of running it.

36
00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:10.309
In the bakery, you and your cousin share one goal, good bread at seven, and you take turns answering the early call.

37
00:02:10.309 --> 00:02:12.074
Teams call that on-call duty.

38
00:02:12.375 --> 00:02:17.054
You make small recipe changes, taste every batch, and watch the café shelf.

39
00:02:17.054 --> 00:02:21.172
Then you buy the timer and the mixer that make those habits cheap.

40
00:02:21.172 --> 00:02:24.854
Teams call those three layers culture, practices and tools.

41
00:02:25.125 --> 00:02:26.689
Notice the order, though.

42
00:02:26.689 --> 00:02:29.629
A shiny new mixer never got two people talking.

43
00:02:29.629 --> 00:02:33.508
Buy every tool and keep the wall, and you still have the wall.

44
00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:35.685
Tools make good habits cheap.

45
00:02:35.685 --> 00:02:37.219
They don't create them.

46
00:02:38.125 --> 00:02:40.369
Now follow one change down the road.

47
00:02:40.369 --> 00:02:42.426
Say you decide to use less sugar.

48
00:02:42.625 --> 00:02:46.546
You write it on the recipe card, bake a test batch, and taste it.

49
00:02:46.546 --> 00:02:51.854
You bag the loaves, the van takes them to the café, and you watch who buys a second one.

50
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.825
Now picture a busy Tuesday.

51
00:02:53.825 --> 00:02:56.664
You skip the tasting, to save ten minutes.

52
00:02:56.875 --> 00:03:01.426
On Friday, an unhappy café owner tells you the bread tastes of nothing.

53
00:03:01.625 --> 00:03:03.741
The mistake was made on Tuesday.

54
00:03:03.741 --> 00:03:06.716
You paid for it on Friday, far down the road.

55
00:03:06.875 --> 00:03:11.430
Hold on to that distance, from where a mistake is made to where it is found.

56
00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:13.288
It is the whole story of today.

57
00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.141
Software takes the same road.

58
00:03:16.141 --> 00:03:22.989
It is called the pipeline: a fixed series of steps, run by machines, that carries a change to real users.

59
00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:23.600
Code.

60
00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:30.325
You change code on your laptop, and commit it to git, a tool that saves snapshots of your files.

61
00:03:30.625 --> 00:03:31.043
Build.

62
00:03:31.043 --> 00:03:34.734
A machine turns the code into something that can run.

63
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:35.330
Test.

64
00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:42.395
Automated tests, small programs that check your program, make sure the change works and broke nothing else.

65
00:03:42.625 --> 00:03:43.181
Package.

66
00:03:43.181 --> 00:03:50.553
The app and everything it needs go into one bundle, a container image, so it runs the same on any machine.

67
00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:51.292
Release.

68
00:03:51.292 --> 00:03:56.779
The package gets a version number, and goes to a shared store, called a registry.

69
00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:57.526
Deploy.

70
00:03:57.526 --> 00:04:01.429
The new version replaces the old one on the servers.

71
00:04:01.625 --> 00:04:01.910
Run.

72
00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:07.249
Software on the servers keeps the app alive, and restarts it if it crashes.

73
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:08.092
Observe.

74
00:04:08.092 --> 00:04:11.716
Graphs, logs and alerts show how the app behaves.

75
00:04:11.875 --> 00:04:12.494
Feedback.

76
00:04:12.494 --> 00:04:15.110
What you learn shapes the next change.

77
00:04:15.375 --> 00:04:16.982
And the loop starts again.

78
00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:21.438
The real, live system that your users use is called production.

79
00:04:21.625 --> 00:04:23.802
And here is the bakery, mapped.

80
00:04:23.802 --> 00:04:25.769
The recipe card is the code.

81
00:04:25.769 --> 00:04:27.454
The tasting is the test.

82
00:04:27.454 --> 00:04:29.070
The bag is the package.

83
00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:30.896
And the van is the deploy.

84
00:04:31.125 --> 00:04:33.911
Let's send one tiny change down this road.

85
00:04:33.911 --> 00:04:41.805
Tomorrow you create hello-devops, a tiny web app that answers every visit with a greeting and a count of visits so far.

86
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.852
You change its greeting from Hello, DevOps!

87
00:04:44.852 --> 00:04:46.312
to Hi there, and push.

88
00:04:46.312 --> 00:04:49.231
But one test still expects the old greeting.

89
00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.024
Where on the road does this mistake get caught?

90
00:04:55.375 --> 00:04:57.495
Right here, at the test step.

91
00:04:57.495 --> 00:04:58.738
It fails at once.

92
00:04:58.875 --> 00:05:04.473
You find out two minutes later, on your own branch, instead of from a user on Friday night.

93
00:05:04.473 --> 00:05:08.595
A branch is your own line of work, kept apart from everyone else's.

94
00:05:08.875 --> 00:05:11.615
Remember the tasting we skipped on Tuesday?

95
00:05:11.615 --> 00:05:15.374
This is that tasting, done by a machine, every single time.

96
00:05:15.625 --> 00:05:21.093
And every step catches a different kind of mistake, as close as possible to where it was made.

97
00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:24.457
Fix the test, push again, and the change moves on.

98
00:05:25.125 --> 00:05:29.206
Steps two to six have a name you will see everywhere: CI/CD.

99
00:05:29.375 --> 00:05:37.083
Continuous integration means you add small changes to the shared code often, and a machine builds and tests each one.

100
00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:43.080
Continuous delivery means every change that passes is ready to release, at any time.

101
00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:44.815
You build both in Part 6.

102
00:05:45.625 --> 00:05:48.035
So, should a machine do everything?

103
00:05:48.035 --> 00:05:48.242
No.

104
00:05:48.242 --> 00:05:50.928
Deciding what to change takes a person.

105
00:05:50.928 --> 00:05:53.545
Building, testing and shipping do not.

106
00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:57.447
When a step goes the same way every time, give it to a machine.

107
00:05:58.125 --> 00:05:59.385
One more guardrail.

108
00:05:59.385 --> 00:06:04.958
Say you change the flour, the oven setting and the baking time, all on the same day.

109
00:06:04.958 --> 00:06:06.550
The bread comes out dry.

110
00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:08.419
Which change did it?

111
00:06:08.419 --> 00:06:09.671
You can't tell.

112
00:06:09.671 --> 00:06:14.343
A release with 300 changes in it can fail in 300 places.

113
00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:17.984
So change one thing at a time, and you will know what broke.

114
00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:21.935
Every part of this course owns one stretch of this road.

115
00:06:21.935 --> 00:06:25.803
When a new tool shows up, come back here and ask which step it owns.

116
00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:29.583
So, is the job done when the code is written?

117
00:06:29.583 --> 00:06:30.269
Not quite.

118
00:06:30.269 --> 00:06:34.243
In a DevOps team, finished code is only the halfway point.

119
00:06:34.375 --> 00:06:38.840
You are done when the change runs well in production, and you can see that it does.

120
00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:40.747
One team owns the whole road.

121
00:06:40.747 --> 00:06:44.542
Every step catches its own mistake, close to where it was made.

122
00:06:44.542 --> 00:06:46.892
And whatever repeats goes to a machine.

123
00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.709
A quick check before you go.

124
00:06:49.709 --> 00:06:55.994
Your team has a script that builds the app and runs the tests, but someone runs it by hand once a week.

125
00:06:55.994 --> 00:06:57.641
What is the best next step?

126
00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:02.400
Run it on every push.

127
00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:05.600
Feedback in minutes beats a tasting once a week.
